The '''2013 FIFA Club World Cup''' (officially known as the '''FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2013 presented by Toyota''' for sponsorship reasons)<ref name="schedule">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/09/75/34/fcwc2013_matchschedule_17122013_neutral.pdf |title=Match Schedule – FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2013 |publisher=FIFA.com |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131219035954/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/09/75/34/fcwc2013_matchschedule_17122013_neutral.pdf |archive-date=19 December 2013 }}</ref> was the 10th edition of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[FIFA]]-organised world club [[association football|football]] tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations as well as the host nation's league champions.<ref name="regulations">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/16/81/19/fcwc2013_regulations_en_neutral.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131008070859/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/02/16/81/19/fcwc2013_regulations_en_neutral.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=8 October 2013|title=Regulations – FIFA Club World Cup Morocco 2013|publisher=FIFA}}</ref> It was hosted by [[Morocco]],<ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5j8XZ6B1erRErqNmo04nPMLK4CMvQ?docId=CNG.cc0deb74231895b25c32fdd9a671f34f.4a1 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140218222622/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5j8XZ6B1erRErqNmo04nPMLK4CMvQ?docId=CNG.cc0deb74231895b25c32fdd9a671f34f.4a1 | url-status = dead | archive-date = 18 February 2014 | title = Morocco to host 2013-2014 Club World Cup | publisher = Google News | agency = Agence France-Presse| date = 17 December 2011 }}</ref> and played from 11 to 21 December 2013.<ref name="schedule"/><ref name=clubwc13>{{cite web |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/fifa-wcc2013.html|title=FIFA Club World Cup 2013|author=Marcelo Leme de Arruda |publisher=[[Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation]]|date=8 September 2016 |access-date=16 December 2018}}</ref>

Defending champions [[Sport Club Corinthians Paulista|Corinthians]] did not qualify as they were eliminated in the round of 16 of the [[2013 Copa Libertadores]]. The eventual winners of that competition, [[Clube Atlético Mineiro|Atlético Mineiro]], were beaten in the semi-finals of the Club World Cup by Moroccan side [[Raja CA|Raja Casablanca]], whose appearance in the [[2013 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]] made them the first club to appear in all four rounds of the competition, having entered in the play-off for the quarter-finals; however, they were unable to make history by winning the title, as European champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] won the final 2–0 for their first Club World Cup title.

==Host bids==
There were four countries bidding to host the 2013 and [[2014 FIFA Club World Cup|2014]] tournaments (same host for both tournaments):<ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.usatoday.com/sports/soccer/2011-05-17-iran-morocco-south-africa-uae-fifa-club-world-cup-bids_N.htm | title = Iran among four bidders to host 2013-14 FIFA Club World Cups | publisher = USA Today | agency = Associated Press| date = 17 May 2011}}</ref>

*{{fba|Iran}}
*{{fba|Morocco}}
*{{fba|South Africa}}
*{{fba|United Arab Emirates}} (which hosted the [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup|2009]] and [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup|2010]] editions in [[Abu Dhabi]])

In October 2011, [[FIFA]] said that Iran, South Africa and the United Arab Emirates all withdrew their bids, leaving Morocco as the only bidder.<ref>{{cite news | url = http://msn.foxsports.com/foxsoccer/soccer/story/Morocco-set-to-host-Club-World-Cup-in-2013-14-99039620 | title = Morocco set to host Club World Cup in 2013, '14 | publisher = FoxSports.com | agency = Associated Press| date = 17 October 2011 }}</ref> The [[FIFA Executive Committee]] officially confirmed Morocco as host on 17 December 2011 during their meeting in [[Tokyo]], Japan.<ref>{{cite web|title=Reform road map speeds up|url=https://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/bodies/news/newsid=1558624/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120104113830/http://www.fifa.com/aboutfifa/organisation/bodies/news/newsid=1558624/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=4 January 2012|publisher=FIFA|date=17 December 2011}}</ref>

